                    <h2 id="tenant-authentication-POSTapi-v1-tenant-auth-token">Sign in and get a token, for clients that cannot hold a session.</h2>

<p>
</p>

<p>The browser SPA uses [login] and a cookie. A phone is launched from an
icon with no cookie and no subdomain, so it carries a token instead and
names its organization in an <code>X-Organization</code> header on every later
request. The token is created in that organization's own database, which
is what makes the header safe to accept: pointed at another
organization, it finds that organization's token table, where this token
does not exist.</p>
<p>The credential check, the rate limit and the lifecycle rules are the same
ones [login] uses -- LoginRequest owns both paths so they cannot drift.
The only difference is that no session is written.</p>

<span id="example-requests-POSTapi-v1-tenant-auth-token">
<blockquote>Example request:</blockquote>


<div class="bash-example">
    <pre><code class="language-bash">curl --request POST \
    "http://localhost:8000/api/v1/tenant/auth/token" \
    --header "Content-Type: application/json" \
    --header "Accept: application/json" \
    --data "{
    \"subdomain\": \"architecto\",
    \"email\": \"zbailey@example.net\",
    \"password\": \"|]|{+-\",
    \"device_name\": \"v\"
}"
</code></pre></div>


<div class="javascript-example">
    <pre><code class="language-javascript">const url = new URL(
    "http://localhost:8000/api/v1/tenant/auth/token"
);

const headers = {
    "Content-Type": "application/json",
    "Accept": "application/json",
};

let body = {
    "subdomain": "architecto",
    "email": "zbailey@example.net",
    "password": "|]|{+-",
    "device_name": "v"
};

fetch(url, {
    method: "POST",
    headers,
    body: JSON.stringify(body),
}).then(response =&gt; response.json());</code></pre></div>

</span>

